Draco actor-ness rests on the aspect alone: drop Actor[DracoType], emit messageType in drake (#48)

Dev's objection: Draco is as much a domain as an actor, so carrying Actor[DracoType]
in the derivation privileges the actor role in the type hierarchy while the domain
role contributes only body members — which contradicts the additive composer's own
premise that every present aspect contributes its block.

The derivation was never what made Draco an actor. isActor (Generator.scala:1721)
matches derivation names ActorType/ExtensibleBehavior, so it was already FALSE for
Draco; actor-ness came from hasActorBehavior — aspect presence — all along. The
derivation was doing one legitimate job (carrying the message type) and one
illegitimate one (the hierarchy). ActorAspect.messageType took over the first (that
element and the aspect-first actorMessageType lookup are in the tree already, but see
the record correction below), so the derivation now goes.

  Draco.json   dracoAspect.derivation dropped -> TypeLoader.rooted supplies DracoType
  Draco.scala  trait Draco extends Actor[DracoType] -> extends DracoType
  Draco.drake  head `type Draco from Actor(DracoType)` -> `type Draco`

PREREQUISITE, done here first: Generator.drake did not emit actorAspect.messageType
(the actor block emitted start/message/signal only), so the message type reached the
drake surface ONLY through the derivation on line 1. Dropping the derivation without
this would have silently made the drake projection lossy — and green, since Draco.json
is not excluded. The actor block now emits `messageType <T>` ahead of the three action
blocks, matching the field order in ActorAspect.drake and Draco.json.

The 12 mods actor definitions still carry Actor[T] derivations and no messageType;
actorMessageType prefers the aspect and falls back to the derivation, so they are
unaffected and migrate later.

